00:39:38.630 --> 00:39:39.620
<v Police Commissioner>Oh.

00:39:39.620 --> 00:40:03.490
<v Chickenman Announcer>Wow. Say, that chicken missile really works nifty. Will the Midland City Fire Department recommend that a chicken missile receiver be installed in what's left of Midland City Hall? Be listening tomorrow for another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crimefighter the world has ever known.

00:40:03.490 --> 00:40:07.950
<v Chickenman>Bock bock bock bock!

00:40:07.950 --> 00:40:09.440
<v Man 1>Chickenman!

00:40:09.440 --> 00:40:14.910
<v Women>He's everywhere, he's everywhere!

00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:19.530
<v Ira Glass>More Chickenman. That all-new episode, coming up.

00:40:19.530 --> 00:40:37.270
<v Ira Glass>In the meantime, my friend Verta tells this story. Verta, Vertamae Grosvenor, is a National Public Radio commentator, host of an NPR show called Seasonings. Anyway, back in 1970, she published a cookbook. And she was interviewed about it on television by Barbara Walters.

00:40:37.270 --> 00:40:47.830
<v Ira Glass>And as Verta tells this story, OK, she's on TV, making fried chicken for Barbara Walters. And at some point, Barbara Walters asks her, "How do you tell the chicken's done?"

00:40:47.830 --> 00:41:03.260
<v Ira Glass>And Verta tells her, "You can tell by the sound." Ms. Walters gives her this look which says, basically, "Give me a break," and quickly cuts to a commercial. And Verta says that's always people's reaction.

00:41:03.260 --> 00:41:16.460
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>They say, "You're crazy, Verta. That's not it." They say, "Tell me something real. What is it? 15 minutes? 20 minutes," or whatever. And I say, "You've got to listen to the sound of the grease. Listen to the music."

00:41:16.460 --> 00:41:43.020
<v Ira Glass>So you have been claiming for years that you can tell if the chicken's done purely by the sound. And so to determine if that's true, we've decided to conduct a little radio experiment here, with your permission. The other night, we fried a chicken, and we recorded it at different stages of its frying. And we are going to play you now four different moments in the frying of the chicken that we've changed the order of.

00:41:43.020 --> 00:41:44.300
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>I'm sure you did, Ira.

00:41:44.300 --> 00:42:06.750
<v Ira Glass>Well, I'm telling you up front. And we want you to listen to the four of them and identify which one is the one where the chicken is done. In other words, can you tell that the chicken is done without any visual cues, without the help of smelling what the chicken is smelling like? Can it be done purely, purely on the basis of sound?

00:42:06.750 --> 00:42:13.330
<v Ira Glass>Let's roll. And our listeners at home can play along with us here. Let's roll the first little sample sound.

00:42:13.330 --> 00:42:15.690
<v Ira Glass>[CHICKEN FRYING]

00:42:15.690 --> 00:42:18.590
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>I would say--

00:42:18.590 --> 00:42:19.110
<v Ira Glass>Yeah?

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:21.350
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>I'd say that's something like the middle.

00:42:21.350 --> 00:42:24.140
<v Ira Glass>OK. But that definitely isn't towards the end, you're saying.

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:28.040
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>Yeah, it's in the middle going toward the end. It's in the three fourths part.

00:42:28.040 --> 00:42:31.810
<v Ira Glass>OK, let's hear sound number two, please.

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:36.970
<v Ira Glass>[CHICKEN FRYING]

00:42:36.970 --> 00:42:39.280
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>Number two is toward the end, too.

00:42:39.280 --> 00:42:41.180
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>OK. Number three.

00:42:41.180 --> 00:42:43.400
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>[CHICKEN FRYING]

00:42:43.400 --> 00:42:46.800
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>I think that's the beginning, more toward the beginning.

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:49.340
<v Ira Glass>That is more toward the beginning. And number four.

00:42:49.340 --> 00:42:50.630
<v Ira Glass>[CHICKEN FRYING]

00:42:50.630 --> 00:42:55.470
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>That's an ender. It's toward the end.

00:42:55.470 --> 00:42:56.020
<v Ira Glass>OK.

00:42:56.020 --> 00:43:02.030
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>When all those little balls are forming on the bottom, those little, nice, crusty.

00:43:02.030 --> 00:43:07.180
<v Ira Glass>Now, Verda, if you would have to hazard a guess as to which one would be the very last one--

00:43:07.180 --> 00:43:10.870
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>I still say it's one or two that's toward the end.

00:43:10.870 --> 00:43:25.600
<v Ira Glass>And the answer is number two, which means that using only the sound of the chicken, Ms. Vertamae Grosvenor can get close to telling when chicken is done, but can't be totally sure of it. In short, this was hard.

00:43:25.600 --> 00:43:29.020
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>Yes, it was hard. You have to see it. Do you know what I mean?

00:43:29.020 --> 00:43:29.510
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.

00:43:29.510 --> 00:43:39.860
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>Oh, it's a serious, labor-intensive thing. You've got to stay on it. You just can't be talking on the phone and watching TV. You've got to stay on that chicken.

00:43:39.860 --> 00:43:44.600
<v Ira Glass>I was asking your daughters today, and they were saying how they can also tell if rice is done by the sound.

00:43:44.600 --> 00:43:53.010
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>Oh yeah, that's true. Yeah, I taught them that. Those are the kind of family values I taught my children. "Listen to the sound of the chicken."

00:43:53.010 --> 00:43:57.100
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>[LAUGHTER]

00:43:57.100 --> 00:44:01.620
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>"Listen to the sound of the rice." You talked to both of them?

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:10.350
<v Ira Glass>I talked to both of them. They were both really funny. One of them said, "Everything that she cooks is golden brown and perfect, perfect, perfect."

00:44:10.350 --> 00:44:13.710
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>Who said that?

00:44:13.710 --> 00:44:15.060
<v Ira Glass>I believe it was Chandra.

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:15.910
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>Really?

00:44:15.910 --> 00:44:27.420
<v Ira Glass>But she said, "But just because we're her daughters, that doesn't mean that she tells us the real recipes. We ask her for the recipes, and tells us recipes. But then when we cook them, they're not the same. And we know that she's holding back on ingredients.

00:44:27.420 --> 00:44:30.890
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>That's-- well, that's not quite true, but you know.

00:44:30.890 --> 00:44:35.130
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>[LAUGHTER]

00:44:35.130 --> 00:44:36.850
<v Ira Glass>That is true. You don't tell them all the ingredients.

00:44:36.850 --> 00:44:42.290
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>Well, no. I just tell them, but then they have to find out the rest for themselves.

00:44:42.290 --> 00:44:42.980
<v Ira Glass>There you go.

00:44:42.980 --> 00:44:44.060
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>You see what I'm saying?

00:44:44.060 --> 00:44:46.040
<v Ira Glass>As with so many things in parenting.

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:57.360
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>I say, "I put a little ginger in." Then they have to figure out how much a little ginger is. That's what they have to do.

00:44:57.360 --> 00:45:15.860
<v Ira Glass>I tell you, going into this, I was completely convinced that you were just going to snap and get it, and it was going to be really easy. But it turned out to be hard without those other cues. And I guess I've learned that the chicken is a tricky thing. Chicken is tricky. Chicken is craftier than we usually give it credit for.

00:45:15.860 --> 00:45:19.250
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>Oh, but did I tell you this one? I have something to tell you about chickens.

00:45:19.250 --> 00:45:20.500
<v Ira Glass>All right, yeah.

00:45:20.500 --> 00:45:54.650
<v Vertamae Grosvenor>The chicken and the rooster had a fight. The chicken knocked the rooster out of sight. Rooster told the chicken, "That's alright. Meet you in the gumbo tomorrow night."

00:45:54.650 --> 00:46:11.950
<v Ira Glass>Well, the fabulous Vertamae Grosvenor from a studio in Washington, DC. More fun a-comin', writer David Sedaris in New York City. And of course, have we mentioned-- I can't remember. Did we mention the new Chickenman episode, the first one since 1969? Did we mention it? All right. Stay with us.

00:46:11.950 --> 00:46:27.010
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "EAT THAT CHICKEN" BY CHARLES MINGUS]

00:46:27.010 --> 00:46:44.860
<v David Sedaris>July 19, 1992. This afternoon, at the 26th Street Flea Market, I had one of those experiences that remind me why I shop in the first place. Not shop like grocery shop, but step out into the world searching for that one thing I cannot name.

00:46:44.860 --> 00:47:04.940
<v David Sedaris>I pass the usual objects, the grinning mammies offering themselves up as salt and pepper shakers, the coffee table made from dice, another head carved from a coconut. "That's collectible," the dealers say, referring to an ashtray in the shape of a doll-sized toilet bowl. Collectible to who?

00:47:04.940 --> 00:47:28.200
<v David Sedaris>Last weekend at the flea market, I saw this thing, a taxidermied turkey's head attached to its own foot. This turkey was equipped with that length of flesh that spills from the top of its beak and fell to its neck. Stiff red hair stood out from the head and shoulders, and the claws were really sharp. You'd think that something armed like that might be able to protect itself.

00:47:28.200 --> 00:47:47.770
<v David Sedaris>I pictured its maker standing by a chopping block saying, "I know. I'll take the turkey's head and attach it to the foot." Why would you do that? Or more importantly, what sort of life would you lead that might enable you to make this connection?

00:47:47.770 --> 00:48:09.440
<v David Sedaris>I was hypnotized by this object and asked the price as if I were under a spell. "$45," the dealer said. My tongue was dry from hanging in the open air, and I tried to fit it back into my mouth. "All right," she said, "$35. $30." But she could have gone up. "All right. $85. $120. $370."

00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:23.120
<v David Sedaris>I had no choice but to follow wherever she led me. I handed over my wallet in a trance, just gave it to her, thinking she could take the whole thing, the cash, blank checks, library card, whatever. Take it all.

00:48:23.120 --> 00:48:36.290
<v David Sedaris>I stared into the face of this taxidermied turkey's head, and nothing else mattered. Tomorrow, what's that? Yesterday doesn't count. My life began the moment I could call this thing my own.

00:48:36.290 --> 00:49:03.380
<v David Sedaris>On the way home I felt giddy and confident that I could approach anyone at all and say, "I'll give you one hundred-- no, $500,000 if you can guess what I've got in this paper sack." And I swear that not one of them could have come up with the right answer. I walked home 30 blocks, looking everyone square in the eye and thinking, "Sucker."

00:49:03.380 --> 00:49:10.600
<v Ira Glass>Well, David Sedaris is the author of Barrel Fever, often heard on NPR's Morning Edition.

00:49:10.600 --> 00:49:32.430
<v Ira Glass>All right. Well, forget about those two new Beatles songs, so-called Beatles songs, put out two and a half decades after the group broke up, because here, here, right here on Your Radio Playhouse , we have chicken mania. Chickenman -ia All right, forget that.

00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:56.490
<v Ira Glass>Dick Orkin, the voice of Chickenman and the brains behind Chickenman. This is the style that launched 1,000 imitators. When I was a kid listening to the radio in Baltimore, I heard this stuff, and really, Chickenman , until the time I was 13 years old, and The Tooth Fairy , another series that Dick Orkin did. And then occasionally, you would hear these commercials for Time magazine or other stuff.

00:49:56.490 --> 00:50:02.480
<v Ira Glass>You could always tell. You could always tell. That's the guy. That's the guy. Those are [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:50:02.480 --> 00:50:28.790
<v Ira Glass>Because they all had this very deadpan feeling. There was just something offbeat. You couldn't even quite put your finger on what it was. And I, like many people who got into radio in the years that followed, spent the early part of my career in radio trying to imitate that sound and then giving up because of the utter futility of that.

00:50:28.790 --> 00:50:56.880
<v Ira Glass>And so we are very, very pleased. All of that is a way of saying we are very, very pleased. We, the corporate we, I and the little production staff here at Your Radio Playhouse, we are very pleased to be able to bring the first new Chickenman episode written since 1969, the first full episode written since 1969. You know, Chickenman is getting up in years. And no one appears to be more aware of that than his creator.

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:05.080
<v Chickenman Announcer>And now, another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crimefighter the world has ever known.

00:51:05.080 --> 00:51:08.780
<v Chickenman>Bock bock bock bock!

00:51:08.780 --> 00:51:11.210
<v Man 1>Chickenman!

00:51:11.210 --> 00:51:13.150
<v Women>He's everywhere, he's everywhere!

00:51:13.150 --> 00:51:14.620
<v Women>["BOCK" ALARM NOISE]

00:51:14.620 --> 00:51:35.600
<v Benton Harbor>Tuesday morning, 9:30 AM. The Chicken Alarm in my Chicken Cave goes off 30 minutes late, which makes me late for my annual physical at my doctor, DuPont Chopper. I didn't want to be late, since it was my first in 30 years. 9:50 AM. I get a flat as I pull the Chicken Coupe out of the Chicken Garage. So I take the convenient number 32 bus, which drops me off only 14 blocks from the doctor's office.

00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:37.910
<v Dupont Chopper>You're late, you're late for a very important date.

00:51:37.910 --> 00:51:41.700
<v Benton Harbor>--says the doctor. And I say, "I know. I'm sorry. All the air went out of my whatchmacallit."

00:51:41.700 --> 00:51:43.700
<v Dupont Chopper>Well, that happens to all of us. Age.

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:46.510
<v Benton Harbor>11 AM. After several tests and poking and prodding--

00:51:46.510 --> 00:51:52.280
<v Dupont Chopper>Well, your cholesterol is a little high. The pain in your toes is arthritis. And I think you have the beginning of a nice hernia.

00:51:52.280 --> 00:51:54.340
<v Benton Harbor>Cholesterol high? I had no idea.

00:51:54.340 --> 00:52:07.290
<v Dupont Chopper>Well, technically, it's not high, because one, it is in your mind. And two, it is your bad cholesterol that's bad. Your good cholesterol is not good. But in time, your bad cholesterol will be good, and your good cholesterol will be bad. And that's not good.

00:52:07.290 --> 00:52:08.260
<v Benton Harbor>Anything else?

00:52:08.260 --> 00:52:09.990
<v Dupont Chopper>Yes, you're ugly.

00:52:09.990 --> 00:52:12.800
<v Benton Harbor>That's my Chicken Mask. The beak and, you know, the feathers.

00:52:12.800 --> 00:52:22.700
<v Dupont Chopper>Uh-huh. Well, don't look at small children or animals. Anyway, that's the health story. But I don't think you have to alter your lifestyle, unless you are a crimefighting superhero of some kind.

00:52:22.700 --> 00:52:24.720
<v Dupont Chopper>[LAUGHTER]

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:26.290
<v Dupont Chopper>Want to buy one of my tapes?

00:52:26.290 --> 00:52:46.370
<v Benton Harbor>12:00 noon. I return to the Chicken Cave. And there, in the chicken mirror-- augh!-- I see something horrible. No, not my ugly face, my own mortality. I ask myself why it never stared me in the face before. After all, I've been fighting crime and/or evil for over 30 years, pursuing it down streets and allies and sewers and other picturesque places. And my mortality never came up even once.

00:52:46.370 --> 00:52:59.870
<v Benton Harbor>1:40 PM. I go see the police commissioner and share with him the doctor's diagnosis. After all, he is my closest friend. And if I can't tell him the bad news, who can I tell? So I give him a new coloring book I bought for him and tell him my sad news. And he says--

00:52:59.870 --> 00:53:03.640
<v Police Commissioner>Ha, ha. This looks like a real neat coloring book.

00:53:03.640 --> 00:53:05.550
<v Benton Harbor>So what do you think commissioner?

00:53:05.550 --> 00:53:06.090
<v Police Commissioner>About what?

00:53:06.090 --> 00:53:07.490
<v Benton Harbor>What I just shared with you.

00:53:07.490 --> 00:53:10.140
<v Police Commissioner>Oh. It's a real neat coloring book.

00:53:10.140 --> 00:53:12.860
<v Benton Harbor>About the diagnosis and the need to alter my lifestyle.

00:53:12.860 --> 00:53:20.530
<v Police Commissioner>Oh. Well, that would mean that you'd have to-- and of course one can't even be sure that that, you know. So anyway, that's how I see it.

00:53:20.530 --> 00:53:21.930
<v Dupont Chopper>OK. Thanks, commissioner.

00:53:21.930 --> 00:53:26.280
<v Police Commissioner>Anytime, winged warrior. After all, what are friends for? And may I ask you a question?

00:53:26.280 --> 00:53:27.050
<v Benton Harbor>OK.

00:53:27.050 --> 00:53:31.430
<v Police Commissioner>If I make the sky blue in this picture and the mountains brown, do you think the rocks should be chartreuse?

00:53:31.430 --> 00:53:32.090
<v Benton Harbor>Perfect.

00:53:32.090 --> 00:53:37.200
<v Benton Harbor>4:00 PM. I go to the local office of the Grace Hill Ferguson Employment Agency and Screen Door Company.

00:53:37.200 --> 00:53:41.870
<v Woman>OK, when you say something more sed-ren-tarry, what do you mean?

00:53:41.870 --> 00:53:44.770
<v Benton Harbor>Sedentary. You know, things where I can seden-- sit.

00:53:44.770 --> 00:53:49.130
<v Woman>Oh, OK. What kind of previous work experience do you have?

00:53:49.130 --> 00:53:52.350
<v Benton Harbor>Basically fighting crime and/or evil.

00:53:52.350 --> 00:53:58.800
<v Woman>And what special equipment, office or otherwise, are you experienced in using?

00:53:58.800 --> 00:53:59.550
<v Benton Harbor>Geshtukna ray gun.

00:53:59.550 --> 00:54:00.720
<v Woman>Could you spell that?

00:54:00.720 --> 00:54:06.040
<v Benton Harbor>G-E-S-H-T-U-K-A-N-A. And I also worked a Chicken Dissolver and a Chicken Modulator and a can opener.

00:54:06.040 --> 00:54:07.590
<v Woman>--T-U-K--

00:54:07.590 --> 00:54:08.140
<v Benton Harbor>--A-N-A.

00:54:08.140 --> 00:54:11.240
<v Woman>OK. Do you know Windows 95?

00:54:11.240 --> 00:54:14.600
<v Benton Harbor>Look, what light job openings are on your list there? Just look.

00:54:14.600 --> 00:54:19.700
<v Woman>Heavy cable puller, refrigerator and piano mover, cement hailer--

00:54:19.700 --> 00:54:20.590
<v Benton Harbor>Hauler.

00:54:20.590 --> 00:54:23.810
<v Woman>OK. Big tree planter! Circus tent--

00:54:23.810 --> 00:54:24.230
<v Benton Harbor>Miss?

00:54:24.230 --> 00:54:24.500
<v Woman>What?

00:54:24.500 --> 00:54:28.030
<v Benton Harbor>Not holler. Those are all active and heavy things. Anything light I could do?

00:54:28.030 --> 00:54:31.140
<v Woman>Yes, a negligee model in a delicatessen.

00:54:31.140 --> 00:54:31.690
<v Benton Harbor>I don't--

00:54:31.690 --> 00:54:37.560
<v Woman>Oh, well scratch that, then. Requires previous experience. Oh, here's something really perfect.

00:54:37.560 --> 00:54:38.150
<v Benton Harbor>What's that?

00:54:38.150 --> 00:54:46.690
<v Woman>A napkin-folder in a nouveau Italian-Chinese restaurant specializing in pizza and light salads. Takeout available. Mmm, I'm hungry.

00:54:46.690 --> 00:54:58.540
<v Benton Harbor>Wednesday morning, 9:30 AM. My Chicken Alarm goes off a half hour late for my geriatric counseling appointment. Tire still flat. So I take the convenient number 18 bus. And that leaves me off only 28 blocks from the counselor's office.

00:54:58.540 --> 00:55:01.270
<v Counselor>I have one word of advice, Mister. Get out.

00:55:01.270 --> 00:55:01.870
<v Benton Harbor>Excuse me?

00:55:01.870 --> 00:55:02.760
<v Counselor>Get out.

00:55:02.760 --> 00:55:04.360
<v Benton Harbor>OK, I'm a few minutes late.

00:55:04.360 --> 00:55:06.400
<v Counselor>What? You're 30 years too late. Get out.

00:55:06.400 --> 00:55:07.960
<v Benton Harbor>Oh, you mean get out, not get out.

00:55:07.960 --> 00:55:12.160
<v Counselor>May I suggest a small, desert, superhero retirement community.

00:55:12.160 --> 00:55:12.830
<v Benton Harbor>Ooh.

00:55:12.830 --> 00:55:13.700
<v Counselor>You play golf?

00:55:13.700 --> 00:55:14.720
<v Benton Harbor>Not real good, no.

00:55:14.720 --> 00:55:20.420
<v Counselor>Good, you'll fit right in. Neither does Fishwoman or that Flying Newt. I sent them there. They love it.

00:55:20.420 --> 00:55:20.660
<v Benton Harbor>Fishwoman?

00:55:20.660 --> 00:55:23.330
<v Counselor>Yeah. Little scaly, nice personality.

00:55:23.330 --> 00:55:24.000
<v Benton Harbor>OK.

00:55:24.000 --> 00:55:25.320
<v Counselor>Want to buy one of my tapes?

00:55:25.320 --> 00:55:40.100
<v Benton Harbor>2:00 AM, next morning. I can't sleep, and I sip warm milk in the Chicken Cave. What to do? I'm on the horns of a dilemma. Heavy concrete hauler? Fold napkins in nouveau Italian-Chinese restaurant specializing in pizza and light salads? Or challenge the fates?

00:55:40.100 --> 00:55:55.810
<v Benton Harbor>8:00 AM. I stand on the roof of Midland City's tallest skyscraper, four dizzy stories high. I hurl a challenge to the fates. This is the winged warrior, fates. I shall go on fighting crime and/or evil. I don't care that my bad cholesterol is bad, that--

00:55:55.810 --> 00:55:58.880
<v Benton Harbor>Suddenly, a black cloud forms swiftly in the sky, and I hear--

00:55:58.880 --> 00:56:07.150
<v Frank The Fate>OK, do what you want. We'll try to be there for you. But watch the fatty foods, the cookies, the ice cream, and good luck to you.

00:56:07.150 --> 00:56:07.990
<v Benton Harbor>Who is that?

00:56:07.990 --> 00:56:08.910
<v Frank The Fate>One of the fates.

00:56:08.910 --> 00:56:09.650
<v Benton Harbor>Which one?

00:56:09.650 --> 00:56:15.670
<v Frank The Fate>Frank. Phyllis, Fran and Fred are in Las Vegas. God bless them, they should only win and be well.

00:56:15.670 --> 00:56:18.520
<v Benton Harbor>OK. Well, fine. I'll just carry on, then.

00:56:18.520 --> 00:56:25.140
<v Frank The Fate>OK. Listen, could you change that mask you're wearing now? It's very ugly and could scare kids and small animals. Thank you very much.

00:56:25.140 --> 00:56:25.710
<v Benton Harbor>Right.

00:56:25.710 --> 00:56:33.400
<v Benton Harbor>And at that moment, I knew fate was blind, and I, the famous fowl, would have the last laugh. For you see, I wasn't wearing a mask.

00:56:33.400 --> 00:56:38.210
<v Benton Harbor>[LAUGHTER]

00:56:38.210 --> 00:57:04.830
<v Chickenman Announcer>Well, what has the winged warrior gotten himself into here? Can he actually stop time by challenging the fates? And is it Frank Fate he's talking to or Georgie Jessel? And another thing, doesn't that nouveau Italian-Chinese restaurant sound super-trendy? Be listening tomorrow for another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crimefighter the world has ever known.

00:57:04.830 --> 00:57:08.700
<v Chickenman>Bock bock bock bock!

00:57:08.700 --> 00:57:10.150
<v Man>Chickenman.

00:57:10.150 --> 00:57:15.000
<v Women>He's everywhere, he's everywhere!

00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:38.880
<v Ira Glass>Well, "Chickenman Challenges a Fate Named Frank" was recorded at Dick Orkin's radio ranch in Hollywood, California. Along with Dick Orkin, the cast included Charlie Brill, Allison Anne Martin, Miriam Flynn, and Jim Gallant. Engineers Elizabeth Lane and James Burns. Written by Dick Orkin and Christine Coyle. Ms. Flynn's hairstyle by Mr. Bunny.

00:57:38.880 --> 00:57:49.190
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]

00:57:49.190 --> 00:58:21.330
<v Ira Glass>The show is produced by Dolores Wilber, Nancy Updike, Peter Clowney, Alix Spiegel and myself. Contributing editors Margi Rochlin, Paul Tough, and Jack Hitt. Many songs in tonight's program were provided by Mr. Steve Cushing and the Blues Before Sunrise Radio Network. Our program comes to you from WBEZ Chicago. Our address, 848 East Grand Avenue, Navy Pier, Chicago, 60611. Our email address, oh why don't you write us, [email protected] . That's [email protected] .

00:58:21.330 --> 00:58:26.000
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGMENTS]

00:58:26.000 --> 00:58:34.120
<v Ira Glass>And finally, thanks to the Mattoon family, Ashley, Lynn, Duki, Yona Lu, and of course, Danielle.

00:58:34.120 --> 00:58:41.490
<v Danielle>I just want a real person who's like Duki, probably. I want to go marry some guy who's like Duki.

00:58:41.490 --> 00:58:43.280
<v Ira Glass>And how's that search going?

00:58:43.280 --> 00:58:44.760
<v Danielle>Not well.

00:58:44.760 --> 00:58:53.660
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. See you next week, same chicken time, same chicken channel. Same brave little radio station.

00:58:53.660 --> 00:58:54.250
<v Danielle>Ira?

00:58:54.250 --> 00:58:55.050
<v Ira Glass>Hmm?

00:58:55.050 --> 00:58:57.870
<v Danielle>Don't make me sound like an idiot, OK?

00:58:57.870 --> 00:59:02.870
<v Ira Glass>Done.
